  <article class="max-w-3xl">
    <h1 class="text-3xl sm:text-4xl font-extrabold text-gray-900 mb-6 tracking-tight">
      How Much Stain Do I Need for a Deck? (Gallons, Railings &amp; Coats)
    </h1>
    
    <div class="prose prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 space-y-5 leading-relaxed">
      <p class="text-lg">
        Calculating stain for an outdoor wood deck is one of the trickiest DIY estimates because railings and vertical spindles consume far more product than the horizontal floorboards. Failing to account for the four exposed sides of balusters or the higher porosity of weathered wood will leave you short on stain midway through the project.
      </p>

      <!-- Calculator CTA -->
      <div class="my-8 p-6 bg-gradient-to-br from-blue-50 to-blue-100 border border-blue-200 rounded-xl shadow-sm">
        <h2 class="text-xl font-bold text-blue-900 mb-2 mt-0">Skip the Geometry: Use Our Free Deck Stain Calculator</h2>
        <p class="text-blue-800 mb-4 text-sm">Enter your deck dimensions, railing linear footage, and wood age to instantly calculate exact gallon cans, 5-gallon contractor pails, and wood cleaner requirements.</p>
        <a href="/calculators/deck-stain" class="inline-block bg-blue-600 hover:bg-blue-700 text-white font-semibold py-2.5 px-5 rounded-lg text-sm transition-colors shadow-xs">
          Open Deck Stain Calculator →
        </a>
      </div>

      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-4">The Universal Deck Stain Coverage Rules</h2>
      <p>
        According to PDCA (Painting and Decorating Contractors of America) industry standards, coverage varies primarily based on wood age and surface texture:
      </p>

      <ul class="list-disc pl-5 space-y-2">
        <li><strong>Smooth / Planed New Lumber:</strong> Covers <strong>200 to 250 sq ft per gallon</strong> on the 1st coat; 300 to 350 sq ft on the 2nd coat.</li>
        <li><strong>Weathered / Aged Gray Wood:</strong> Covers <strong>150 to 175 sq ft per gallon</strong> because dry wood fibers thirstily soak up stain.</li>
        <li><strong>Rough-Sawn Cedar:</strong> Covers <strong>125 to 150 sq ft per gallon</strong> due to deep surface texture.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-4">The 2.5x Railing &amp; Spindle Multiplier</h2>
      <p>
        Never calculate railings as simple flat square footage. Because each vertical spindle (baluster) has four exposed faces, top rail bevels, and bottom channel edges, standard painting estimator practice is to multiply the linear footage of your perimeter railing by <strong>2.5 to 3.0</strong> to determine true paintable square footage:
      </p>

      <div class="bg-slate-900 text-slate-100 p-4 rounded-xl font-mono text-sm my-4 text-center">
        Railing Paintable Area (sq ft) = Perimeter Railing Length (ft) × 2.5
      </div>

      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-4">Deck Stain Estimating Chart</h2>
      <div class="overflow-x-auto my-4">
        <table class="min-w-full text-xs text-left border border-gray-200 rounded-lg">
          <thead class="bg-gray-100 font-bold text-gray-800">
            <tr>
              <th class="p-3 border-b">Deck Dimensions</th>
              <th class="p-3 border-b">Floor Area</th>
              <th class="p-3 border-b">Railing (3-Sided)</th>
              <th class="p-3 border-b">Total 3D Area</th>
              <th class="p-3 border-b">Stain (2 Coats)</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody class="divide-y divide-gray-200 text-gray-700">
            <tr><td class="p-3 font-semibold">10 ft × 12 ft</td><td class="p-3">120 sq ft</td><td class="p-3">32 ft (80 sq ft)</td><td class="p-3">200 sq ft</td><td class="p-3 font-bold text-blue-700">2 Gallons</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="p-3 font-semibold">12 ft × 16 ft</td><td class="p-3">192 sq ft</td><td class="p-3">40 ft (100 sq ft)</td><td class="p-3">292 sq ft</td><td class="p-3 font-bold text-blue-700">3 Gallons</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="p-3 font-semibold">16 ft × 20 ft</td><td class="p-3">320 sq ft</td><td class="p-3">52 ft (130 sq ft)</td><td class="p-3">450 sq ft</td><td class="p-3 font-bold text-blue-700">4 Gallons (or 1× 5-Gal Pail)</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="p-3 font-semibold">16 ft × 24 ft</td><td class="p-3">384 sq ft</td><td class="p-3">56 ft (140 sq ft)</td><td class="p-3">524 sq ft</td><td class="p-3 font-bold text-blue-700">5 Gallons (1× 5-Gal Pail)</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="p-3 font-semibold">20 ft × 30 ft</td><td class="p-3">600 sq ft</td><td class="p-3">70 ft (175 sq ft)</td><td class="p-3">775 sq ft</td><td class="p-3 font-bold text-blue-700">7 Gallons (1× 5-Gal + 2 Cans)</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

